***Nebula Browser*** is a web browser for Apple TV devices running tvOS. It uses private API's that Apple normally does not allow in App Store distributed apps. Highlights:
- Uses WKWebView for fast, modern website rendering.
- Browser tabs with a focus-driven overview.
- Modern Liquid Glass UI with selectable accent themes (the app icon follows the theme).
- A built-in full screen video player with fast-forward, scrubbing and a transport bar.
- In-player WebVTT subtitles, real-event page clicks that work inside sandboxed frames, and a favorites-grid landing page (this fork).
- Multiple search engines, page zoom, and privacy options.

This code is provided as is with no warranty or liability. Use at your own risk and do ***not*** distribute builds of this project on the App Store.


Credits
=============

Nebula Browser was created by **Alan Maldonado**, developed with the help of AI ([Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)): the full screen video player transport controls (fast-forward, scrubbing, transport bar, resume), the back/double-back navigation, the arrow button shortcuts, the per-page video fullscreen, the privacy options, and many input-handling fixes were built in collaboration with it. It is based on the open-source *tvOS Browser* by Steven Troughton-Smith and Jip van Akker.

The streaming-playback changes in this fork (native fullscreen engine, in-player WebVTT subtitles, trusted native clicks, favorites landing page) were developed in collaboration with **Hermes Agent (Nous Research)**. Original authorship and the upstream project remain as above — this fork adds, it does not rename or rebrand.

Upstream issues and feature requests belong at [alan-maldonado/appletv-nebula-browser](https://github.com/alan-maldonado/appletv-nebula-browser). Thank you Alan! 🌌


How to Install Nebula Browser
=============

Upstream ships no binary; building from Xcode (as described below) is the general path. This fork additionally publishes **developer-signed builds** under Releases — they install only on devices registered in the signer's provisioning profile (e.g. `xcrun devicectl device install app --device <AppleTV-UDID> <file>.ipa`). Not notarized, not for the App Store.

To install this app, connect your Apple TV to your macOS computer via WiFi (or USB on older devices). Open this project in Xcode and install to your Apple TV.
Connecting to your Apple TV wirelessly: http://www.redmondpie.com/how-to-wirelessly-connect-apple-tv-4k-to-xcode-on-mac/.

How to Use Nebula Browser
=============

Quick start:
- Double press the center of the touch area to switch between cursor & scroll mode. In scroll mode, press Back or Select to return to cursor mode.
- Press the touch area while in cursor mode to click. Double-tap a video to send it full screen.
- Single tap the Menu button to go back. Tap it twice within 2 seconds at the start of history to exit the app.
- Single tap Play/Pause for the Quick Menu: enter a URL or search, reload, go forward, jump to a site's root ("Go to <domain>"), or open Settings.
- Double tap Play/Pause to open the full Settings menu. Adjustable settings (marked ‹ ›) change with the Left/Right keys, with an explanation shown beside the menu.

Arrow buttons while browsing:
- Single press Up/Down: scroll the page (Up at the very top focuses the toolbar).
- Single press Left/Right: scroll horizontally.
- Double press: Up = Tabs, Down = Favorites, Left = History, Right = New Tab. (Can be turned off in Settings.)

Tabs overview:
- Swipe left/right to move between tab cards, press the touch area to switch tabs.
- Swipe up (or tap Play/Pause) to close the focused tab, with an app-switcher style animation.
- Tap Menu to close the overview.

Video:
- Videos play inline on the page. Double-tap a video, **hold Play/Pause**, or use the page's own full screen control to open it in the built-in full screen player.
- While in the full screen player, press **Menu** to hand playback back to the page — the page video re-attaches and keeps playing (no reload), and the page's auto-resume tricks can't fight the handover.
- When a direct media URL is available it opens in the built-in full screen player; otherwise it expands within the page.
- **WebVTT subtitles** shown by the page carry into the full screen player automatically (yellow with outline; some sites render them larger).
- Full screen player: press Right to fast-forward at 2x → 32x, Left to slow down, Play/Pause to resume from that point. Left/Right while paused skip ±10s. Swipe to scrub (quick swipes accelerate). Back cancels a fast-forward/scrub and returns to where you were. Videos resume where you left off.
- A "Full Screen Player" option in Settings can make videos always open in the built-in player instead of inline (off by default).
- Buttons, modals and in-frame controls respond to real clicks from the remote — including inside cross-origin frames that scripted clicks cannot reach.

Streaming playback compatibility (this fork):
These are generic web-standards fixes — they help the browser handle well-behaved and badly-behaved video pages alike, for content you are entitled to view:
- **Full-screen handover:** opening a page video into the native player, and handing playback back to the page on Menu, without a page reload; a short escalating re-attach recovers live streams whose edge momentarily stalls.
- **Single-decoder rule:** while the native player owns playback the page's media elements are held paused — some pages auto-resume after any pause, which used to run two decoders at once and stutter 1 GB devices.
- **Header policy for media requests:** correct User-Agent/Referer handling, and no cross-host credential-style headers on redirects, which standards-compliant CDNs require.
- **Live media handling:** no-cache playlist fetches, correct handling of byte-range and mislabeled media segments, so HLS live windows advance properly.
- **Subtitles:** WebVTT subtitle tracks shown by a page are carried into the native full-screen player.
- **Responsiveness:** interactive page hit-testing degrades fast on pathologically heavy pages instead of delaying the remote.

Settings (Side menu) include:
- Navigation: home page (default is a favorites-grid landing; any page can be set as home, reset from the menu), favorites, history, tabs, new tab, arrow shortcuts on/off.
- Appearance: accent theme, top navigation bar, page scaling, font size.
- Video Playback: full screen player, player time display (remaining / total).
- Search: search engine (Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo, Yahoo, Ecosia, Brave, Startpage).
- Privacy: what to open on launch (last session / home page), clear history on exit, privacy mode (hide content in the app switcher).
- Compatibility: desktop / mobile user agent.
- Maintenance: clear cache, clear cookies.
- Help: usage guide, show guide on launch.
